feat(deploy): replace the object store and put its Filer behind the gateway (#227)

The store the reference deployment ran was archived upstream and delisted from
the registry this repository pulled it from, so a clean machine could not fetch
it at all. The replacement is maintained and Apache-2.0, and was measured
against SEG-005 under the configuration the stack actually uses -- bucket
default rule set at creation, the sealer setting no retention of its own, all
eight checks green, and a sealed segment inheriting COMPLIANCE from the bucket
and refusing its own writer's versioned delete.

Driven by OPS-029 and OPS-030, proposed in doc 07 first and both observed
failing: the bucket init that shipped with the old store cannot run against
this one, and the Filer's unauthenticated DELETE destroyed a COMPLIANCE-retained
object that the S3 layer had just refused to delete for the store's own root
identity.

THE FILER IS A SECOND DOOR TO THE SAME BYTES. Object lock is enforced at the S3
layer; the Filer speaks its own protocol to the same metadata and takes no
credential. So the store is three containers rather than one -- bytes, metadata,
gateway -- the Filer runs with its HTTP listener switched off, and it sits on a
network whose only other member is the gateway. OPS-029 attempts that delete
from the network the sealer and the canary are on and requires it to find
nothing, having first shown the same request destroying a retained object in
the one-process arrangement, so the refusal is the isolation and not an absent
API.

RM-144's narrowing survives, expressed differently. This store has no separate
permission for setting a bucket's object-lock configuration, so an identity
granted a bucket-wide write may downgrade COMPLIANCE to GOVERNANCE. A
prefix-scoped write grant is refused that call, and refused creating a bucket,
deleting one, suspending versioning, putting a bucket policy or lifecycle rule,
shortening an object's retention and deleting a retained version under a
bypass, while writing every object the sealer and the canary write. OPS-026
measures both halves; the grant it replaces could write anywhere in the bucket
and this one cannot.

The bucket init and scripts/backup-ledger.sh now drive the reference S3 client
rather than the client that shipped with the removed store, and every assertion
reads the configuration back off the server. A create call that reports success
is a claim about a command and not about a bucket.

Also measured and worked around: a write that cannot be placed because the
store has no writable volume left is returned to the client as HTTP 500, which
in these harnesses reads exactly like object lock refusing a write. Each bucket
reserves seven volumes and the default cap is eight, so the cap is raised where
more than one bucket is made.

